# {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}

The `{{ .Container }}` worker of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}: an SQS
queue whose messages an AWS Lambda function in TypeScript consumes in
batches, keeping the items they carry in a DynamoDB table, with its
infrastructure as a CDK app. Scaffolded by theos.

## The queue

A producer sends each message to the queue (the stack's `EntryPoint`, its
URL) over TLS, with its own AWS credentials allowed `sqs:SendMessage` on the
queue (the stack's `QueueArn`). A message's body is one item:

```json
{ "id": "lamp-1", "name": "Lamp", "quantity": 3 }
```

An id is 1 to 64 letters, digits, `-` or `_`. A name is 1 to 200 characters;
a quantity a whole number, 0 or more. The worker stores the item under its
id, replacing what was there. SQS delivers a message at least once; one
delivered again with the same message id is stored once. A message that
fails, a malformed body or an error storing it, is delivered again alone,
not with its batch, and after five deliveries waits in the dead-letter queue
for 14 days, where an alarm tells you; `docs/runbook.md` says how to send it
back.

## Develop

Open the repository in its devcontainer, which has Node 24 and the Go toolchain.

```sh
npm ci           # install the exact packages in package-lock.json
npm test         # unit tests and the stacks' assertions, with vitest
npm run synth    # write the development CloudFormation templates into cdk.out/
{{- if .Standard }}
make check       # what CI runs: format, lint, type check, test, synth, cfn-lint
make help        # every target
{{- end }}
```

Nothing here deploys or needs an AWS account: synth runs offline, bundles the
function with the installed esbuild, and needs neither Docker nor credentials.

## Tiers and operations

The stacks are built for a tier, `production` or `development`, which is the
place they run, not the code: `make synth` writes both, into
`cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and a deploy picks one
(`cdk deploy -c tier=production --all`; without `-c tier` it is `development`).
The production stack is `{{ .Container }}`, the development one
`{{ .Container }}-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes
production.
Production keeps the tables and queues when the stack goes, protects the tables and the stack
from deletion, recovers them to any second of the last 35 days, backs them up
daily with AWS Backup, traces with X-Ray and keeps logs a year; development
throws its data and messages away with the stack and keeps logs a month. Both tiers alarm
and have a dashboard.

What the app promises whatever the tier, the backups' retention and copy
region, the recovery objectives and who the alarms tell, is in
`infra/operations.ts`, which theos renders from the Container's
`spec.operations`: change the block, and the file follows it. The alert email is the
stack's `AlertEmail` parameter, which a deploy can set per environment. Each
stack outputs `EntryPoint`, the queue's URL, `QueueArn`, its ARN, and
`AlarmTopicArn`, the topic its alarms notify.

Deploying (`cdk deploy`, after `cdk bootstrap` of the account) is for its
owner, with their own credentials.

## Layout

- `src/handler.ts` is the Lambda entry point: it creates the DynamoDB client
  and Powertools' Logger and Metrics once, at module scope, reads
  `TABLE_NAME` and `IDEMPOTENCY_TABLE_NAME`, and hands each batch to
  `consumeBatch` in `src/batch.ts`, which runs Powertools' batch processing
  and answers the messages that failed.
- `src/consume.ts` checks a message and stores its item; `src/handler.ts`
  runs that through Powertools' Idempotency, after the check; `src/items.ts` holds the rules, free of AWS;
  `src/item_store.ts` keeps items in DynamoDB behind the `ItemStore`
  interface tests fake.
- `infra/stacks.ts` defines the stacks for a tier; `infra/queue_stack.ts` is
  the main one: the queue and its dead-letter queue, the tables, the
  function with its log group, role and event source, the alarms
  (`infra/alarms.ts`) and, in production, the backups (`infra/backups.ts`).
  The stack is named after the package. `infra/operations.ts` holds what
  the Container promises.
- `infra/connectors.ts` lists the data stores the Container's Relationships
  connect it to, which theos renders from their Connectors; the stack
  connects each to the function, alarms on it and backs it up.
{{- if has .Connectors "dynamodb" }} The
  `dynamodb` DataStore's table is made by `infra/connector_dynamodb.ts`, and
  the function reads its name in `DYNAMODB_TABLE_NAME`. New data goes in that
  table: the `Items` table (`TABLE_NAME`) is the starter's example.
{{- end }}
{{- if has .Connectors "rds" }} The
  `rds` DataStore's Aurora PostgreSQL cluster is made by
  `infra/connector_rds.ts`, and the function reads `RDS_CLUSTER_ARN`,
  `RDS_SECRET_ARN` and `RDS_DATABASE`. Query it through the Data API:
  `@aws-sdk/client-rds-data`'s
  `ExecuteStatementCommand({ resourceArn, secretArn, database, sql })`, the
  first three from those variables. The IAM database user is a migration
  this repository runs.
{{- end }}
{{- if .Standard }}
- `infra/main.ts` is the CDK app `cdk synth` runs (`cdk.json`), and
  `infra/standard.ts` says what each tier means, tags every stack and adds
  CDK Nag's checks. `docs/runbook.md` says how to restore the data, roll a
  deployment back, and what to do when an alarm fires.

Read `AGENTS.md` before changing anything: it is the rulebook, for people and agents.
{{- end }}
